.TH CVEC_APPEND_ARRAY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_append_array \- append a C array to a vector (type-inferred, fatal on failure)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_append_array(v, arr_ptr, elem_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a wrapper for cvector_append_array(). It calls
ccol_fatal_err() when the append fails. A caller with no useful recovery
for an allocation failure therefore does not need to test a return value.

The macro copies elem_count elements from arr_ptr onto the end of v. It
does this in one bulk copy, and it grows the capacity one time at most. It
does not grow the capacity once for each element. The append is all or
nothing. Either every element lands in the vector, or the vector stays
exactly as it was.

The macro copies the elements byte for byte from arr_ptr. arr_ptr must
therefore point at elements of the declared element type of the vector.
This macro converts no element. cvec_push(3) does such a conversion, but
this macro does not.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v           Vector to append to
arr_ptr     Pointer to the first element of the array to copy from. It
            can point at a const array.
elem_count  Number of elements to copy
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program with ccol_fatal_err() when the append fails.
The append fails when the allocation fails. It fails when the new element
count is more than ccol_max_elem_count. It fails when elem_count is large
enough to overflow that count. It fails when arr_ptr is NULL and
elem_count is not zero. It also fails when arr_ptr aliases the backing
buffer of v and elem_count reads past the end of the reservation of that
buffer.
An elem_count of zero succeeds. It leaves the vector unchanged, and it
leaves arr_ptr unchanged. The macro therefore rejects a NULL arr_ptr only
when there is something to copy.
The macro evaluates elem_count exactly once, on every path. This includes
the failure path. An expression with a side effect is therefore safe to
pass.
arr_ptr can point into the backing store of v. An example is a pointer
from cvec_data_ptr(3) or from cvec_at_ptr(3). A capacity growth that this
call starts does not invalidate such a pointer. The source range and the
destination range can also overlap. The source range must stay inside the
reservation of that buffer.
The capacity grows one time at most for each call. It grows to the next
power of two at or above the new element count, with a floor of 4.
The macro asserts when v is NULL.
Use cvector_append_array(3) directly to handle a failed append yourself.
That function does not stop the program.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int src[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_append_array(vec, src, 5);
// cvec_size(vec) == 5
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_append_array (3),
.BR cvec_append_cvec (3),
.BR cvec_reserve (3),
.BR cvec_push (3)
